<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<sdf version="1.9">
  <model name="projectile">
    <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
    <link name="link">
      <!-- Gravity OFF so the ball hangs motionless from the instant it is
           created until it is thrown. The `gz` CLI costs ~0.5 s of SIM time
           per call, so with gravity on the ball free-fell 1.55 m and hit the
           ground before its impulse arrived, then rolled away along z=0.040
           and never crossed the camera frustum (measured 2026-07-26).
           spawn_projectile restores gravity as a persistent wrench of
           -mass*g on /world/<world>/wrench/persistent, published from the
           same warm publisher as the launch impulse so both land within a
           step or two and the parabola stays exact (ballistics.py, kalman.py
           both assume a = -g). -->
      <gravity>false</gravity>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.150</mass>  <!-- 150 g — baseball-class -->
        <inertia>
          <!-- Solid sphere: I = 2/5 * m * r^2 = 2/5 * 0.15 * 0.04^2 -->
          <ixx>9.6e-5</ixx><iyy>9.6e-5</iyy><izz>9.6e-5</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>

      <!-- NO <collision> ELEMENT, DELIBERATELY. The ball must collide with
           NOTHING: it flies its parabola through the airframe and the runway
           alike. This is a measurement decision, not a fidelity shortcut. A
           150 g ball at 8-14 m/s does tumble a 3.5" quad, and it did: 2026-07-26
           the battery scored B03 r0 as dodged=True min=0.27 m (a real
           miss-distance failure), then the contact flipped the vehicle,
           ArduPilot crash-checked (AngErr=170>30) and disarmed, and every
           remaining run aborted on MIN_SPAWN_Z with the wreck on the ground.
           One marginal dodge destroyed the other ~50 runs, and a flipped
           vehicle cannot be recovered in-harness because ArduPilot refuses to
           arm inverted.

           That was first "fixed" with <collide_bitmask>0x00</collide_bitmask>
           written as a child of <surface>. It belongs to <surface><contact>, so
           SDFormat DISCARDED it and the ball kept colliding. Gazebo said so on
           every single spawn and nobody was reading:

             Warning [Utils.cc:132] [.../surface/collide_bitmask:model.sdf:L47]:
             XML Element[collide_bitmask], child of element[surface], not
             defined in SDF. Copying[collide_bitmask] as children of [surface].

           Measured cost (2026-08-07, cell r12.0 dataflash 00000008.BIN): six of
           eight throws produced an UNCOMMANDED attitude excursion starting
           0.27-0.47 s after the dodge (the tca window, i.e. ball arrival),
           peaking at a roll of 33-48 deg, past ATC_ANGLE_MAX=30 and so not
           commandable. Severity tracked ball speed: 8 m/s -> 17 deg, 14 m/s ->
           20-38 deg, 20 m/s -> 48 deg, and the last rolled to 180 deg and
           crashed. Every sweep cell died this way, which is why the S20N
           false-dodge controls have no data at any detection range.

           Removing the element outright, rather than moving the bitmask to its
           correct parent, is the point: a link with no collision geometry
           cannot collide under ANY physics engine, so this cannot regress into
           a silently-ignored attribute a second time. Dynamics are unaffected:
           the launch impulse and the gravity wrench act on <inertial>, and the
           depth detector renders <visual>.

           Nothing is lost: hits are scored from min_dist_m, computed
           geometrically from /gz/dynamic_poses against hit_radius, so the
           verdict is unchanged and better resolved than "did the physics engine
           flip it". To restore contact for a deliberate impact-damage study,
           re-add a <collision> with a sphere of radius 0.040, and expect one
           scored run per stack restart. test_projectile_model.py pins this. -->

      <visual name="visual">
        <geometry>
          <sphere><radius>0.040</radius></sphere>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.8 0.1 0.1 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.8 0.1 0.1 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
